<Base
  title="Loyalty and the whole world of men"
  description="Iliad Books 16 and 18: Patroclus dies in Achilles’ armour, Achilles returns, and the shield shows cities at peace and at war."
>
  <LessonChrome slug="loyalty-and-the-world">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>Loyalty and the whole world of men</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Today we add loyalty to the foundation. Honor already has a house. It now
        has a friend, and a picture of the world that is not only a battle.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="learn" aria-labelledby="will-learn">
      <h2 id="will-learn">What you’ll learn</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Patroclus weeps for the Greeks and asks to wear Achilles’ armour. He does not know he is asking for his own death.</li>
        <li>Zeus grants that he will drive the Trojans from the ships, and refuses to let him come safely out of the fight.</li>
        <li>When Patroclus falls, Achilles’ grief ends the withdrawal. He says he will force his soul into subjection and go after Hector.</li>
        <li>The shield Hephaestus makes shows two cities, a lawsuit, ploughing, harvest, and a dance. The warrior’s world is larger than the war.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="lesson-body">
      <p class="scene-today">Today we add loyalty to the foundation.</p>
      <h3>The friend who asks to go</h3>
      <p>
        Let’s begin at the ships. Patroclus comes to Achilles in tears. Achilles
        compares him to a child clinging to her mother. Patroclus says he weeps
        for the Argives. Their champions are wounded. He asks whether Achilles is
        still so inexorable, and what later story will say of him if he does not
        save them. Then he asks for the armour, and for the Myrmidons. The poem
        says he did not know what he was asking, nor that he was suing for his own
        destruction.
      </p>
      <p>
        Achilles is cut to the heart that a man of his own rank robbed him because
        he was stronger. He still will not go himself. He tells Patroclus to gird
        on the armour and lead the Myrmidons, to drive the Trojans from the ships,
        and not to push the fight on toward the city. Patroclus arms. He does not
        take the great Pelian spear. Only Achilles can wield it. Achilles prays to
        Zeus. Zeus hears, and grants only half. Patroclus will thrust the war back
        from the ships. He will not come safely out.
      </p>
      <p>
        Learn the shape of the loyalty. It is not a mood beside the code. It is
        part of the code. Patroclus goes because the men at the ships are dying,
        and because Achilles will not. He wears the other man’s armour so that the
        fight can be done in that man’s name. The glory he tells the Myrmidons to
        win is “glory for the son of Peleus.” The friend spends himself for the
        honor of the man who stayed behind.
      </p>

      <h3>Grief, and the return</h3>
      <p>
        Antilochus brings the news. Achilles takes dust in both hands and pours it
        over his head. The women they had taken captive scream and beat their
        breasts. Antilochus holds his hands, afraid he will cut his own throat.
        Achilles tells his mother that Zeus has fulfilled the prayer, and that it
        boots him nothing, because Patroclus has fallen, the man he valued more
        than all others and loved as dearly as his own life. He will not live or
        go about among mankind unless Hector falls by his spear.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then he says the sentence that closes the withdrawal. Perish strife, and
        anger, which rises in the soul like smoke and tastes sweeter than honey.
        Even so Agamemnon angered him. And yet, so be it, for it is over. He will
        force his soul into subjection as he needs must. He will go. He will
        pursue Hector, and then abide his own doom. The honor of Lesson 2 kept him
        out. Loyalty, and the death it cost, brings him back. The price of the
        friend’s going is the thing Achilles loved most.
      </p>

      <h3>A world worked on the shield</h3>
      <p>
        Thetis goes to Hephaestus — Butler’s Vulcan — for new armour. The shield
        is made in five thicknesses. On it are two cities. In one there are
        weddings, torchlight, the cry of Hymen, and dancing. In the same city the
        people gather in assembly over a quarrel: blood-money for a man who has
        been killed. One man says he has paid in full. The other says he has not.
        The elders sit on seats of stone, staves in their hands, and each gives
        judgment. Two talents lie there for the judge whose judgment is deemed
        fairest.
      </p>
      <p>
        Around the other city two armies are camped, divided whether to sack it or
        to take half and spare it. Then a ploughed field, a harvest with reapers
        and binders, and a dancing-floor like the one Daedalus made at Cnossus for
        Ariadne. The rim is the river Oceanus. Read that list against the war you
        have been in since Book 1. The poem stops, in the middle of the making of
        a weapon, and shows peace, law, farming, and a feast of movement. The
        warrior’s world is bigger than the killing. The shield says so while the
        man who will wear it is getting ready to kill Hector.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a professional, the useful point is the order of these facts. Loyalty
        can cost the person one loves most, and it can also be the thing that ends
        a proud withdrawal. And the work of a life, even a warrior’s, contains a
        lawsuit, a field, and a wedding. If your own work has narrowed to the
        quarrel, the shield is the reminder the poem itself inserts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Reader
      heading="The text: Iliad, Book 16"
      note={note}
      sourceHref={book16.source}
      paragraphs={book16.paragraphs}
      glossary={[
        { word: "Patroclus", note: "Achilles’ companion. He will wear Achilles’ armour and die in it." },
        { word: "Myrmidons", note: "Achilles’ own men." },
        { word: "Jove", note: "Butler’s name for Zeus." },
      ]}
    />

    <Reader
      heading="The text: Iliad, Book 18"
      note={`${note} The shield begins where Hephaestus shapes it and sets two cities on it.`}
      sourceHref={book18.source}
      paragraphs={book18.paragraphs}
      glossary={[
        { word: "Vulcan", note: "Butler’s name for Hephaestus, who makes the shield." },
        { word: "Hector", note: "He has killed Patroclus and taken the armour." },
      ]}
    />

    <section class="word-day" aria-labelledby="word-day">
      <h2 id="word-day">Word of the day</h2>
      <p class="word-greek">comrade</p>
      <p>
        Butler’s word, in Book 18, is “comrade,” and Achilles says he loved
        Patroclus as dearly as his own life. This lesson does not add a Greek
        line for that sentence. The course’s name for the step is loyalty: the
        bond that sends Patroclus out, and that brings Achilles back. Find
        “comrade” and “my own life” in the second reader.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Reveal
      items={[
        {
          prompt: "What does Patroclus ask, and what does he not know?",
          answer:
            "He asks to wear Achilles’ armour and lead the Myrmidons. The poem says he does not know he is asking for his own destruction.",
        },
        {
          prompt: "What does Zeus grant, and what does he refuse?",
          answer:
            "He grants that Patroclus will drive the war back from the ships. He refuses to let him come safely out of the fight.",
        },
        {
          prompt: "What does Achilles say about anger once he has heard of the death?",
          answer:
            "He says anger tastes sweeter than honey, that Agamemnon angered him, and that it is over. He will force his soul into subjection, pursue Hector, and then meet his own doom.",
        },
        {
          prompt: "Name three things on the shield that are not the battle at Troy.",
          answer:
            "A city with weddings; a lawsuit over blood-money, with elders giving judgment; a ploughed field, or the harvest, or the dancing-floor.",
        },
      ]}
    />

    <section class="reflection" aria-labelledby="reflect-title">
      <h2 id="reflect-title">A short reflection</h2>
      <p>
        If you want a sentence: whose work have you carried when they would not
        go themselves, and what did it cost? The poem has already told Patroclus’s
        story. Yours stays in this browser.
      </p>
      <label class="reflection-label" for="note-loyalty">Your note</label>
      <textarea id="note-loyalty" data-note="loyalty-and-the-world" rows="4"></textarea>
    </section>

    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Honor, duty, and now loyalty. Patroclus goes in another man’s armour because the ships are burning and Achilles will not.</li>
        <li>Zeus’s half-grant is the poem’s way of saying the errand succeeds and the man does not survive it.</li>
        <li>The withdrawal in Lesson 2 ends here, not because the gifts in Lesson 4 were enough, but because the friend is dead.</li>
        <li>The shield puts a lawsuit, a field, and a wedding on the same object as a city under siege. The code was never only the fight.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">
        Loyalty completes the warrior’s bond with one other person, and it can
        cost that person. The shield, made for the man who is about to return,
        insists that the world he belongs to is wider than his revenge.
      </p>
    </section>

